# openmux.env.example - template for a site's FTDI identity strings.
#
# Copy it, fill in what differs, hand it to the build:
#
#   cp openmux.env.example acme.env      # edit acme.env
#   ./build.sh --buildspec acme.yaml --openmux-env acme.env
#
# What it changes: the factory menu programs each chip's USB manufacturer and
# product strings from the values in this file, read at flash time by
# /opt/openmux-factory-tools/*.py off one fixed path in the booted image
# (/boot/firmware/openmux.env). Precedence is flag > process env > this file >
# the constant in the script, so every key you leave out keeps the shipped
# constant - which means a partial file is normal, and an uncommented key is a
# decision rather than an oversight.
#
# It affects the INSTALL MEDIUM's factory menu, and the chips it programs. A
# running OpenMux console never reads this file: at boot the ports come from
# /etc/udev/rules.d/99-openmux-ftdi.rules matching the serial already written in
# the chip, so changing the strings here does not rename any /dev/openmux port.
#
# Leave every key commented out and the build refuses the file ("no KEY=VALUE
# lines"), on purpose: a template that shipped unchanged would produce a medium
# that advertises a custom identity while flashing the stock one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The reader is a KEY=VALUE loop with no shell in it (load_env_file() in
# scan_ftdi.py), which fixes the format:
#
#   * comments only on their own line. Everything after the first '=' is the
#     value, so `OPENMUX_MANUFACTURER=ACME  # vendor` sets `ACME  # vendor`.
#   * quotes are STRIPPED from each end and are never part of the value, so they
#     are optional and quote nothing: `"ACME"` sets ACME. That also means a
#     quote at the end of a value is silently eaten (`ACME"Mfg"` sets ACME"Mfg),
#     and one anywhere inside survives into manufacturer="..." in the
#     ftdi_eeprom conf. Write the value bare and it means what you typed.
#   * no `export`, no `$VAR`, no line continuation, and no key twice: a repeated
#     key keeps the LAST line and the earlier one disappears, which is not what
#     the editor of the first one meant.
#   * upper-case KEY, one per line, ASCII printable only: the value ends up in a
#     USB descriptor that other people's machines display.
#
# build/check-openmux-env.py checks all of this against the shipped parser and
# the shipped udev rules before the medium is built, and prints the value each
# tool will actually use. Run it by hand any time:
#
#   python3 build/check-openmux-env.py acme.env
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Manufacturer string written to EVERY chip this medium programs - console and
# EX-12 alike. This is what a host's device manager shows first.
#OPENMUX_MANUFACTURER=ACME A/S

# Product string of the console's own two chips (the USB a host sees as COM
# ports, and the debug serial).
#OPENMUX_PRODUCT_INTERNAL=ACME Console HW8, FTDI

# Product string of an EX-12 adapter's three chips.
#OPENMUX_PRODUCT_EXTERNAL=ACME eXtention EX-12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two keys BELOW ARE NOT A RENAME - leave them alone unless the udev rules
# are changing in the same release.
#
# A serial prefix is matched LITERALLY by 99-openmux-ftdi.rules (OMC[0-9]*-A),
# by openmux-detect-hardware.sh (shell case OMC*-A) and by the defaults in
# scan_ftdi.py - three files, two packages, all of which have to move together.
# This file cannot change a rule, so setting a new prefix here alone means: the
# flash succeeds, the chip re-enumerates as e.g. ABC1234-A, no rule claims that
# serial, and the board has no /dev/openmux ports at all - which looks exactly
# like a dead card. build/check-openmux-env.py compares these two keys against
# the rules the same build ships and refuses a mismatch.
#
# Default prefix for the console's own chips: OMC (OpenMux Console) ->
# /dev/openmux/port1-8, flat.
#OPENMUX_SERIAL_PREFIX_INTERNAL=OMC
#
# Default prefix for a plug-in adapter's chips: OMX (OpenMux eXtention) ->
# /dev/openmux/<serial>/port1-12, one folder per card.
#OPENMUX_SERIAL_PREFIX_EXTERNAL=OMX
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# After flashing a chip with a medium built from this file, confirm both ends:
#
#   /opt/openmux-factory-tools/scan-ftdi-adapters.py           # configured prefixes
#   /opt/openmux-factory-tools/scan-ftdi-adapters.py --serials # what is programmed
#
# and read one chip back from a host, to see the strings as a customer will.
